Here's a complete implementation for a C# MCP server wrapping a REST API:

---

**Program.cs** (stdio transport):

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace); // stderr only — stdout is for JSON-RPC

builder.Services.AddHttpClient("api", client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.example.com");
    client.DefaultRequestHeaders.Add("Accept", "application/json");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

---

**ApiTools.cs** (tool class wrapping a REST API):

```csharp
using System.ComponentModel;
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;

[McpServerToolType]
public class ApiTools(IHttpClientFactory httpFactory, ILogger<ApiTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Fetch a user profile by ID from the REST API.")]
    public async Task<string> GetUser(
        [Description("The unique user identifier")] string userId,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching user {UserId}", userId);
        var client = httpFactory.CreateClient("api");
        return await client.GetStringAsync($"/users/{userId}", cancellationToken);
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("Search users by name. Returns JSON array of matches.")]
    public async Task<string> SearchUsers(
        [Description("Name or partial name to search for")] string query,
        [Description("Max results to return (1-50)")] int limit = 10,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var client = httpFactory.CreateClient("api");
        var response = await client.GetAsync(
            $"/users?q={Uri.EscapeDataString(query)}&limit={limit}", cancellationToken);
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(cancellationToken);
    }
}
```

---

**How DI works for MCP tools:**

The SDK uses two injection mechanisms:

1. **Constructor injection** — DI services (`IHttpClientFactory`, `ILogger<T>`, your own services) are injected via the class constructor. Use a non-static class with a primary constructor as shown above.

2. **Method parameter injection** — Special framework types (`CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, `IProgress<ProgressNotificationValue>`) are injected per-call into the method. These **never** appear in the tool's JSON schema exposed to the LLM.

The key rule: any parameter the SDK recognizes as a service or framework type is injected silently. Only parameters with `[Description]` attributes become part of the tool schema that the LLM sees.

---

**To scaffold and run:**

```bash
dotnet new mcpserver -n MyApiServer
cd MyApiServer
# Replace generated files with the above
dotnet build && dotnet run
```

The server will wait on stdin for JSON-RPC messages (the MCP protocol). Clients like VS Code or GitHub Copilot launch it as a subprocess.
